<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  RUT_TheScald.xml                       UtinniPatches · authored 2026-09-06
  ============================================================================
  LIQUID_BIOMES_MAP_1 — OFFLINE HALF. The item's "boiling ocean". Reconciled
  against world/ASHKARR_WORLDMAP_tiles.csv: region "Scald" is 312 tiles,
  biome=Lake, water=1 on all 312 (MEASURED via
  ~/.claude/skills/measuring-large-artifacts, sha256:b38fd68569237c96),
  matching design/Jawa/worldbuilding/ASHKARR_WORLD_DEFINITION.md's Scald
  table (centre (35,185), radius 10.5, −30 m ruled / −350 m live-measured,
  "a terminal pan" — eight rivers end in it, none leaves).

  🔴 NOT ParentName="Lake". Verified by direct read of
  Core/Defs/BiomeDefs/Biomes_Water.xml: the <BiomeDef> tag for Lake (and for
  Ocean, used by RUT_TwilightSea.xml/RUT_GreySea.xml in this same folder)
  carries no Name="..." attribute at all — same trap RUT_NightsideIce.xml
  already found and documented for IceSheet. ParentName="Lake" would
  silently fail to resolve, so this restates Lake's shape directly instead.

  Why a NEW BiomeDef rather than patching vanilla Lake in place: Lake is not
  the Scald alone. The same CSV shows a second, unrelated region ("Damp",
  10 tiles) also painted biome=Lake — patching Lake's waterDeepTerrain etc.
  in place would boil that lake too, which no design doc asks for. A
  dedicated def is the only way to give the Scald its own water terrain
  without collateral damage elsewhere on the planet.

  What this def carries:
  - isWaterBiome/canBuildBase/allowRoads/allowRivers/hasVirtualPlants/
    impassable/isBackgroundBiome/workerClass/texture: copied verbatim from
    vanilla Lake (same file, lines 19-32) — nothing here changes the
    baseline world-map behaviour, only the water terrain underneath it.
  - waterDeepTerrain/waterShallowTerrain/waterMovingShallowTerrain/
    waterMovingChestDeepTerrain -> the RUT_ScaldWater* terrains authored
    alongside this file (RUT_ScaldWater.xml), carrying the boiling-lift
    mechanic from design/Jawa/mods/REGROWTH_BOILING_LIFT_SPEC.md.
    oceanDeepTerrain/oceanShallowTerrain deliberately left UNSET (inherit
    vanilla WaterOceanDeep/Shallow): the Scald is Lake-typed, not
    Ocean-typed, so these fields are never queried for it; the matching
    RUT_ScaldWaterOcean{Deep,Shallow} terrains exist anyway (R-B3 ruled all
    six slots authored as a set) and sit ready if a future tile ever needs
    them.
  - wildAnimals / coastalWildAnimals / fishTypes: left EMPTY, documented
    here rather than invented. Two independent reasons, not one:
      1. No biome definition sheet exists for the Scald at all (checked
         design/Jawa/worldbuilding/biomes/ — no the_scald.md, no
         the_anvil.md; terminator_sea.md's own comparison table only uses
         "the Scald" as a foil for the terminator seas, it does not define
         the Scald's own roster). Inventing a creature list without an
         owner-reviewed sheet is exactly the kind of call
         "Biome sheets are a conversation loop" says is his, not mine.
      2. design/Jawa/worldbuilding/the_seas.md (Lane 1) already names
         repatriation candidates for the seas generally — KwazelMaw, Mott,
         Dianoga, Dragonsnake, Fambaa, Fanback, Blixus — but assigns them to
         "Lake/DesertOasis/coastal biomes" as a class, not to the Scald by
         name, and that assignment is SEAS_WATERLINE_PASS_1's job, a
         separate, already-scoped item. Populating wildAnimals here ahead of
         that item would fork the roster decision into two places.
  README_BIOME_GRAMMAR.md's table currently reads "terminator_sea.md | the
  three seas | done" — that line overclaims for the Scald specifically and
  should be corrected once a real Scald sheet exists (not done in this
  pass; flagged in the reconciliation note this item also writes).
  ============================================================================
-->
<Defs>

  <BiomeDef>
    <defName>RUT_TheScald</defName>
    <label>the Scald</label>
    <description>A perched ocean in the world's hottest basin, held in a vast crater by the ring of mountains that feed it. It boils where it is shallow and simmers where it is deep, and it spills outward through every river that nourishes the world.</description>
    <workerClass>BiomeWorker_Ocean</workerClass>
    <isWaterBiome>true</isWaterBiome>
    <canBuildBase>false</canBuildBase>
    <!-- allowRoads TRUE by owner ruling 2026-09-07: "All of our biomes should allow roads." 91 authored road links crossed RUT_NightsideIce and were invisible - the links were structurally valid (asymmetric 0, nonAdjacent 0), the biome simply refused to draw them. -->
    <allowRoads>true</allowRoads>
    <allowRivers>false</allowRivers>
    <hasVirtualPlants>false</hasVirtualPlants>
    <texture>World/Biomes/Ocean</texture>
    <impassable>true</impassable>
    <isBackgroundBiome>true</isBackgroundBiome>
    <waterShallowTerrain>RUT_ScaldWaterShallow</waterShallowTerrain>
    <waterDeepTerrain>RUT_ScaldWaterDeep</waterDeepTerrain>
    <waterMovingShallowTerrain>RUT_ScaldWaterMovingShallow</waterMovingShallowTerrain>
    <waterMovingChestDeepTerrain>RUT_ScaldWaterMovingChestDeep</waterMovingChestDeepTerrain>

    <!-- SEA_FLOOR_AND_CATCH_PASS_1, 2026-09-23. Makes the land tiles around the
         Scald read as coastal and cut a real shore, with the Scald's OWN water
         rather than vanilla ocean. The two terrains are named explicitly because
         the four fields above are the FRESH-water pair this def uses inland; the
         terrain its own map lays is RUT_ScaldWaterOceanDeep. MayRequire on the
         <li> is load-bearing: an unresolvable extension class discards the whole
         BiomeDef silently. -->
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="RimMandrake.SeaShores.RM_SeaShoreExtension" MayRequire="mandrake.rm.seashores">
        <deepTerrain>RUT_ScaldWaterOceanDeep</deepTerrain>
        <shallowTerrain>RUT_ScaldWaterOceanShallow</shallowTerrain>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>
    <!-- WILD ANIMALS, landed 2026-09-09 by BIOME_FAUNA_ASSIGNMENT_SITTING_1 from
         design/Jawa/worldbuilding/biomes/rosters/the_scald.json. This supersedes the
         "no Scald sheet exists, so wildAnimals is left empty" note above - the_scald.md
         now exists and is frozen, and it names these bottom-walkers.
         Both are the sando lineage (adult + elder) walking the crater floor at depth
         where the boil gentles, which is what satisfies ban 4; the icon carve-out is
         why they keep their names. Their shipped `predator` flag is stripped by
         BiomeFaunaStatAdjustments_Generated.xml, per the same roster.
         🔴 No <li>: the node NAME is the animal, the node TEXT is the commonality.
         ⚠️ No animalDensity was declared and the engine default is 0, so neither
         sando could ever spawn. CLOSED 2026-09-09 (fix wave,
         BIOME_FAUNA_ASSIGNMENT_SITTING_1): 0.15 declared below. -->
    <wildAnimals>
      <RSW_SandoAquaMonster MayRequire="mandrake.rsw.swbestiary">0.03</RSW_SandoAquaMonster>   <!-- sando aqua monster - bottom-walker adult, import -->
      <RSW_ElderSando MayRequire="mandrake.rsw.swbestiary">0.005</RSW_ElderSando>              <!-- elder sando - bottom-walker elder, import -->
      <!-- ECOSYSTEM_PYRAMID_LAW_1 (owner, 2026-09-20: "50% small"): this roster read
           0.0% small against the two sando bottom-walkers. Both additions are already
           OWNER-RULED members of this exact biome's fauna list
           (design/Jawa/worldbuilding/biomes/rosters/the_scald.json "fauna", "import",
           0.5 each) that were never wired — not invented. RSW_Faa (bodySize 0.2) and
           RSW_Mee (bodySize 0.15) are this mod's own defs, same package the sando pair
           above already gates on. -->
      <RSW_Faa MayRequire="mandrake.rsw.swbestiary">0.5</RSW_Faa>  <!-- faa - small underwater resident, ruled -->
      <RSW_Mee MayRequire="mandrake.rsw.swbestiary">0.5</RSW_Mee>  <!-- mee - small underwater resident, ruled -->
    </wildAnimals>
    <!-- animalDensity: undeclared (engine default 0) -> 0.15, DESIGNED interim
         (2026-09-09 fix wave). the_scald.md is NOT a terminator sea (prep §6.5): it is
         a hot, productive crater whose quiet floor is where the boil gentles - a
         living bottom, so it sits ABOVE the two cold terminator seas (0.1) and below
         vanilla IceSheet's 0.2 (both MEASURED from the def dump). ⚠️ A quicktest tune
         is OWED. -->
    <animalDensity>0.15</animalDensity>
    <coastalWildAnimals />

    <!-- FISH_BESTIARY_BUILD_1, wave 1 (2026-09-18) — the margin harvest,
         fish_bestiary_commission_2026-09-10.md §2E. MEASURED, not the design
         doc's own "UNMEASURED" flag: RUT_ScaldWater.xml's Deep/Shallow/
         MovingShallow/MovingChestDeep terrains (the ones actually wired to
         this def's own waterDeepTerrain/waterShallowTerrain/
         waterMovingShallowTerrain/waterMovingChestDeepTerrain fields above)
         inherit `waterBodyType Freshwater` from Core's own WaterDeepBase/
         WaterShallowBase/WaterChestDeepBase — verified by direct RimSage
         read, not assumed. So this is freshwater_Common/freshwater_Uncommon,
         not the design doc's placeholder "_Common"/"_Uncommon" naming.
         Added directly to this def (not a separate patch file) because this
         mod already owns RUT_TheScald outright, same convention the
         wildAnimals block above already used.
         maxFishPopulation 30: INVENTED, a third of the Cracked Lands' 90 "in
         spirit" per the design doc's own ⚑ — a margin harvest, not a river. -->
    <!-- SEA_FLOOR_AND_CATCH_PASS_1, 2026-09-24 — extended per the owner's sea
         catch brief (design/Jawa/worldbuilding/sea_catch_rosters_2026-09-24.md):
         three NEW catches (doss, thuum, ekkel) bring the table from five to
         eight and add the eel/alien kinds it lacked plus a second
         crustacean. maxFishPopulation 30 -> 40 for the larger table; still
         the smallest fishery on the planet, a margin harvest. -->
    <maxFishPopulation>40</maxFishPopulation>
    <fishTypes MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Odyssey">
      <freshwater_Common>
        <RUT_Eesh>1.5</RUT_Eesh>
        <RUT_Muddal>0.8</RUT_Muddal>
        <RUT_Doss>0.7</RUT_Doss>
        <RUT_Thuum>0.5</RUT_Thuum>
      </freshwater_Common>
      <freshwater_Uncommon>
        <RUT_Karrash>1</RUT_Karrash>
        <RUT_Saal>0.5</RUT_Saal>
        <RUT_BladderboilCatch>0.5</RUT_BladderboilCatch>
        <RUT_Ekkel>0.4</RUT_Ekkel>
      </freshwater_Uncommon>
      <rareCatchesSetMaker>RUT_RareScaldCatches</rareCatchesSetMaker>
    </fishTypes>

    <!-- GRAVTIDE SUPPORT, added 2026-09-07 (UNDERWATER_BIOME_SUPPORT_1).
         GravTide's arrival gate is generic - OceanArrivalPatches.IsOceanTile and
         OceanTileFeatures.IsWater both test PrimaryBiome.isWaterBiome - so this sea
         already qualifies for a gravship landing and a dive. What it lacked is the
         three things GravTide patches onto vanilla Ocean/Lake BY defName, which never
         reach a modded biome:
           1. terrainsByFertility - THE CRITICAL ONE. MapGenUtility.TerrainFrom() finds
              no terrain without it, logs "No terrain found in biome <X>" and falls back
              to TerrainDefOf.Sand, which is why an unpatched water biome generates an
              all-SAND map instead of a sea.
           2. baseWeatherCommonalities - otherwise every landing logs
              "All weather commonalities were zero. Defaulting to Clear."
           3. wildAnimalsCanWanderInto false - land animals have nowhere to walk from.
         Depth comes from NEGATIVE elevation (OceanTileFeatures.DepthOf = max(0,-elev));
         these tiles sit at -350 m, so ~350 m: past the ~150 m high-pressure nervous
         syndrome threshold, inside drysuit range (800 m). -->
    <wildAnimalsCanWanderInto>false</wildAnimalsCanWanderInto>
    <baseWeatherCommonalities>
      <Clear>14</Clear>
      <Fog>8</Fog>
    </baseWeatherCommonalities>
    <terrainsByFertility>
      <li>
        <terrain>RUT_ScaldWaterOceanDeep</terrain>
        <min>-999</min>
        <max>999</max>
      </li>
    </terrainsByFertility>
  </BiomeDef>

</Defs>
